2. Scope and System Boundaries

2.1 Ship boundary

The Ship model begins at the deorbit decision. This boundary is chosen because the dominant contributors to outcome probability for an orbital-return vehicle arise upstream of the terminal area: heat-shield health, the deorbit burn, and entry dispersions. The model includes the capture fittings located below the forward flaps, propellant slosh, residual translational and angular velocity at contact, engine cutoff on contact, and post-capture support. It does not model the entry aerothermodynamics in detail; it carries their effect as a distribution over arrival states at the terminal area.

2.2 Booster boundary

The Booster model begins at stage separation. It includes grid-fin hardpoints, boostback and landing-burn dispersions, propellant mass uncertainty, and the capture and abort envelopes. The boundary is chosen because the dominant upstream uncertainty for the Booster is propulsion and propellant management during boostback and landing burn, not entry.

2.3 Unified treatment

A unified model is deferred. Shared ground infrastructure does not imply shared vehicle interfaces, and merging the two chains before each is independently characterized would allow assumptions valid for one vehicle class to propagate silently into the other.

2.4 Terminal boundary

Each chain terminates in one of the five outcome classes defined in §5.1, or in an explicitly modeled abort trajectory whose admissibility has been assessed under its own state estimate and envelope.

