3. Evidence Base and State of the Art

3.1 Provenance tiers

Every parameter and every causal claim carries a tier.

Tier Class Permitted use
A Primary source or direct measurement Reference value with stated uncertainty
B Peer-reviewed Model input
C Credible secondary reporting Preliminary, with sensitivity analysis
D Unverified, conflicting, or reconstructed Exploratory only; interval, not point

A tier is a property of the evidence, not of the claim's importance. A parameter central to the conclusion does not become Tier A because the conclusion needs it.

3.2 The reference architecture

The tower-capture architecture is treated as a complete system: tower, actuated arms, vehicle hardpoints, relative positioning, damping, and stabilization. Public dimensional and tolerance figures for this system are Tier C or D unless supported by a primary source. In particular, the geometric protrusion of the Ship fittings is recorded as approximately 0.3 m at Tier D; no public primary source establishes the load capacity of an individual fitting, and no load figure is used as a design input.

3.3 Recent flight evidence

A recent Ship recovery demonstrated that the terminal chain can function: the vehicle was engaged on two fittings below the forward flaps, with the final engine shutting down on contact. The same campaign produced a Booster anomaly in which the boostback burn terminated early following a low propellant-mass alarm, with engine performance identified as the proximate cause; guidance subsequently executed an automated divert and the flight termination system destroyed the vehicle offshore. This sequence is a correct abort, and it is treated as such: it is a safety-system success and a full economic loss, and the two are recorded separately.

The initial public attribution of the Booster anomaly to guidance was revised within a day to a propellant and engine-performance attribution. The framework does not treat either attribution as settled; both are carried in the claim ledger with dates and supersession links.

3.4 Capture family taxonomy

Six families are distinguished.

A — Rigid capture. A stiff interface with minimal compliance; capture requires arrival within a narrow geometric envelope.

B — Compliant capture. A deliberately compliant interface that trades stroke for peak load reduction.

C — Active platform. A capture surface whose position is actuated during the terminal phase to meet the vehicle.

D — Distributed multi-point capture. Capture at several separated hardpoints, with load sharing across them.

E — Passive self-centering. Geometric guides that convert arrival offset into centering force.

F — Flexible cable or net. A reconfigurable capture surface carried by cables or a net, positioned by actuated carriers.

3.5 The net-capture benchmark

A net-based Booster recovery system has been demonstrated downrange. Its published configuration comprises four cables carried by rail-mounted dollies, dynamically positioned and guided by lidar to engage hooks on the Booster body, with a capture frame of approximately 54 m by 54 m on a tower of approximately 67 m. The frame dimensions, tower height, and the use of rail-mounted lidar-guided dollies are Tier C. The claim that lidar is mounted at each corner of the frame is not uniformly reported and is retained at Tier D.

Tolerance figures for this system conflict. One secondary source states that landing position errors of up to 10 m are tolerated and contrasts this with millimetre-scale alignment for rigid capture. Other reporting describes hook engagement with the Booster several metres off centre. A further secondary source reports tens of metres of tolerance in two directions, and some sources cite ±50 m. Official reporting states only that the system has strong adaptability to landing deviations and effectively enlarges the capture window, without a numerical figure.

These figures are not the same quantity. A position error at capture is not a pin-in-slot alignment requirement, and contrasting them directly is a category error. The framework therefore does not compare tolerance numbers across families. It compares admissible arrival-state sets.

3.6 The admissible arrival-state set

For a capture architecture \mathcal{A}, the admissible arrival-state set is

\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{A}}=\left\{x_0\in\mathcal{X}:\ \exists\,u(\cdot)\in\mathcal{U}\ \text{such that capture succeeds and support is stable}\right\}

where x_0 is the vehicle state at the start of the terminal engagement window, u(\cdot) is an admissible control and actuation trajectory, and the success condition is evaluated by the same criteria for every family. The set is a property of the architecture, the controller, and the environment distribution, and it is the correct basis for comparison because it absorbs the mechanism's own actuation capability instead of reducing it to a single precision figure.

For the net system, \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{A}} is recorded as an interval whose endpoints are not jointly determined by the conflicting public sources. The conflict is retained in the ledger rather than resolved by selecting one figure.

3.7 Runtime assurance precedent and its limits

The Simplex architecture establishes the pattern used here: an independent monitor detects imminent violation of a safety property, and a switching logic transfers authority to a verified backup. Runtime assurance has been standardized for unmanned aircraft and extended to several safety-critical domains.

An in-orbit flight of a neural-network controller with watchdog and guardrail runtime assurance has been reported. That demonstration was conducted in shadow mode on a small satellite attitude-control task. It establishes that such an architecture can be flown; it does not establish latency under authority, false-negative rates under switching, or transferability to a decision whose horizon is on the order of seconds and whose consequence is the loss of a vehicle and a tower. It is cited here as a feasibility existence proof and for nothing further.

3.8 Slosh evidence and its limits

Experimental work on fluid–vehicle interaction during touchdown has characterized how residual propellant affects landing stability and structural response, varying horizontal landing velocity and fill level on a sub-scale touchdown demonstrator. That work validates a model form at small scale. It does not validate full-scale vehicle parameters, and the extrapolation from demonstrator to flight vehicle is a stated limitation, not an assumption. The demonstrator's mass limit is recorded as a quantity to be verified from the primary text; it is not used as a confirmed numerical constraint here.

The same literature cites a two-dimensional multibody treatment in which a rigid pendulum represents the sloshing fluid attached to a rigid lander model under lunar gravity. That treatment is the closest published model-form comparison available and is adopted as one candidate form, not as the definitive one.

