4. Mathematical Framework

4.1 State definition

In planar reduction, the state is q=[x,\,z,\,\theta,\,\alpha]^{\mathsf T}, where (x,z) is the centre-of-mass position, \theta the pitch attitude, and \alpha the equivalent slosh generalized coordinate. In spatial reduction, the state comprises six rigid-body degrees of freedom plus slosh and flexible generalized coordinates.

4.2 Variable-mass rigid-body dynamics

The translational and rotational equations are

m(t)\,\dot{\mathbf v}=\mathbf T+\mathbf F_{\text{aero}}+m(t)\mathbf g+\mathbf F_{c}

\mathbf I(t)\dot{\boldsymbol\omega}+\dot{\mathbf I}\boldsymbol\omega+\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf I\boldsymbol\omega=\mathbf M_{T}+\mathbf M_{\text{aero}}+\mathbf M_{c}+\mathbf M_{\text{jet}}+\mathbf M_{\text{slosh}}

where \mathbf T is the thrust force including momentum flux and pressure thrust, \mathbf M_{\text{jet}} is the jet-damping moment, and m(t), \mathbf I(t), and the centre-of-mass location are time-varying. The propellant momentum flux must be accounted for exactly once: it enters \mathbf T as the momentum flux term and must not be double-counted in \mathbf M_{\text{slosh}} or in the rigid-body inertia derivative. This accounting is verified analytically in the limit of zero slosh before any coupled simulation is run.

4.3 Slosh excitation: vector formulation

The excitation of the sloshing fluid is not a scalar magnitude. It depends on the apparent acceleration field in the tank frame, its direction relative to the tank axes, the vehicle angular velocity, and the angular acceleration.

Define the external specific force

\mathbf a_{\text{ext}}(t)=\frac{\mathbf T+\mathbf F_{\text{aero}}+\mathbf F_{c}}{m(t)}

and express it in the tank-fixed frame with rotation matrix \mathbf R(t) mapping inertial to tank coordinates:

\mathbf a_{\text{app}}(t)=\mathbf R(t)^{\mathsf T}\left(\mathbf a_{\text{ext}}(t)-\mathbf g\right)

The apparent acceleration felt by the fluid includes the rotational contribution:

\mathbf a_{\text{app}}^{\text{rot}}(t)=\mathbf a_{\text{app}}(t)-\dot{\boldsymbol\omega}\times\mathbf r-\boldsymbol\omega\times\left(\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf r\right)-2\boldsymbol\omega\times\dot{\mathbf r}

where \mathbf r locates the fluid element in the tank frame. The slosh generalized force is then the projection of \mathbf a_{\text{app}}^{\text{rot}} onto the model's sensitive axes, not the norm of \mathbf a_{\text{ext}}.

A scalar magnitude \|\mathbf a_{\text{ext}}\|/m may be retained only as a conservative amplitude envelope for bounding, and must be labelled as such. It is not a sufficient excitation for a directionally sensitive slosh model.

4.4 Slosh model-form comparison

Three candidate forms are carried.

Pendulum. With generalized coordinate \alpha and tank-frame projection a_{\perp} perpendicular to the nominal fluid surface,

m_s l_s^2\ddot\alpha+c_s\dot\alpha+m_s\,a_{\text{app},\perp}(t)\,l_s\sin\alpha=-m_s l_s\,a_{\perp}^{\text{drive}}(t)\cos\alpha

The coefficients are re-derived by Lagrange before use; the damping coefficient c_s is a calibrated interval, not a theoretical placeholder.

Spring-mass. A lumped translational oscillator whose frequency and damping are matched to the first antisymmetric mode.

Reduced computational fluid dynamics. A reduced-order model calibrated against the sub-scale demonstrator data, used to test whether the lumped forms misestimate amplitude or phase.

The procedure is: (i) global sensitivity on fill level, impact velocity, and c_s across the parameter box; (ii) if slosh is non-negligible in the sensitivity, comparison of the three forms against the sub-scale demonstrator data; (iii) propagation of the model-form discrepancy as an explicit uncertainty, not as a resolved choice. The two-dimensional rigid-pendulum multibody treatment under lunar gravity provides a fourth reference point for model form, with the caveat that its gravity regime differs from the terminal descent regime considered here.

4.5 Contact law

Contact is governed by Signorini conditions on each candidate contact i:

g_i\ge 0,\qquad \lambda_i\ge 0,\qquad g_i\lambda_i=0

closed by a compliant normal law of Hunt–Crossley type:

F_{n,i}=K_i\,\delta_i^{\,n}\left[1+\frac{3\left(1-c_{r,i}\right)}{2}\frac{\dot\delta_i}{\dot\delta_i^{(-)}}\right],\qquad F_{n,i}\leftarrow\max\left(0,F_{n,i}\right)

where \delta_i is penetration, c_{r,i}\in[0,1] is the coefficient of restitution, and \dot\delta_i^{(-)} is the approach velocity at the instant of contact initiation. The damping parameter is

\lambda_i=\frac{3\left(1-c_{r,i}\right)}{2}\frac{K_i}{\dot\delta_i^{(-)}}

so that its units follow from those of K_i and \dot\delta_i^{(-)}, and its sign is fixed by the sign convention of \dot\delta_i. The construction has two properties that matter: the damping force vanishes at \delta_i=0, so no non-physical tensile force is generated at contact initiation; and the \max(0,\cdot) clamp prevents adhesion during unloading. These properties are verified analytically in the single-contact limit before any multi-contact simulation is run.

Tangential contact uses regularized Coulomb friction with a regularization parameter that is itself an interval. A restitution-based impulse law is used in separate runs, and the two contact treatments are compared on the same scenarios so that the choice of contact law does not silently determine the conclusion.

4.6 Capture sequence and load transfer

Contact-detection latency \tau_d and engine thrust tail-off are modelled explicitly:

T(t)=T_0\,\phi\left(t-t_c-\tau_d\right)

where t_c is the nominal contact instant and \phi is a monotonically decaying cutoff function. During the transfer interval, the vertical equilibrium is

T(t)+\sum_i F_{z,i}(t)\approx m(t)\,g

until support is established. The transfer interval is where the assumption that contact loads are weight-dominated rather than impact-dominated is either valid or not, and the framework does not assume it holds.

4.7 Post-contact motion: rigid-body equations with contact patches

The post-contact motion is governed by the full rigid-body equations of §4.2. The fittings enter as two unilateral contact patches i=1,2, each with clearance g_{h,i}, stiffness K_i, damping coefficient c_i, and friction coefficient \mu_i, inside the contact law of §4.5. The resultant contact force and moment are

\mathbf F_{c}=\sum_{i=1}^{2}\mathbf F_{n,i}+\mathbf F_{t,i},\qquad \mathbf M_{c}=\sum_{i=1}^{2}\mathbf r_i\times\left(\mathbf F_{n,i}+\mathbf F_{t,i}\right)

No separate hinge degree of freedom is introduced. The effective rocking axis is a result of which patch is closed, not an input. Because the contact law is piecewise and the clearance is an interval, the rocking response may exhibit non-smooth bifurcations; the sensitivity analysis of §10 checks the clearance parameter explicitly rather than averaging over it inside a single Monte Carlo pass.

4.8 Energy partition

The residual energy at contact is

E_{\text{res}}=\tfrac{1}{2}m\|\mathbf v\|^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\boldsymbol\omega^{\mathsf T}\mathbf I\boldsymbol\omega

and it is partitioned among vehicle deformation, tower deformation, damping, control action, and post-contact oscillation. A linear interface model F=kx+c\dot x is retained only as a first approximation; the nonlinear contact law of §4.5 is the operative model, and the linear model is used solely to establish analytic limits.

