5. Outcome Tree and Decision Logic

5.1 The five outcome classes

A recovered or non-recovered trajectory terminates in exactly one of five classes:

1. Failure to reach the recovery envelope.
2. Deliberate abort with the vehicle expended.
3. Deliberate abort with recovery potential.
4. Mechanical capture failure.
5. Capture followed by unstable support.

Classes 2 and 3 are distinct. Class 2 is a safety success and a full economic loss. Class 3 is a safety success with partial economic recovery. Class 1 is not an abort and does not receive abort credit. Classes 4 and 5 are capture failures with distinct consequences: class 5 includes captures that appear successful and then degrade, and it is the class most likely to be underweighted by a model that treats contact as the terminal event.

5.2 Three separate probabilities

The framework defines three quantities and never interchanges them.

P_{\text{reach}}=P\left(\text{reach recoverable envelope}\right)

P_{\text{rec}}=P\left(\text{capture}\wedge\text{stable support}\right)

P_{\text{safe}}=P_{\text{rec}}+P\left(\text{abort}\wedge\text{admissible}\right)

The abort term is itself split:

P\left(\text{abort}\wedge\text{admissible}\right)=P_{2}+P_{3}

with P_2 the probability of class 2 and P_3 the probability of class 3.

At the leaf level the classes are mutually exclusive: a trajectory that reaches the envelope either proceeds to a capture attempt or to a deliberate abort, and a capture attempt terminates in stable support, capture failure, or unstable support. Mutual exclusivity is stated at the leaf level, not at the level of intermediate events, and the tree is constructed so that no trajectory can be counted twice.

P_{\text{rec}} and P_{\text{safe}} are separate objectives. They are not merged. An architecture that shifts probability mass from capture to admissible abort raises P_{\text{safe}} while lowering P_{\text{rec}}, and the two effects are reported separately so that the trade is visible rather than absorbed.

5.3 Decision rule with abort risk in both branches

Let p=P\left(\text{safe capture}\mid\hat x\right) be the estimated conditional capture probability. Let C_U be the cost of an unsafe capture attempt, V_R the value lost by refusing a feasible capture, and let R_{ab\mid GO} and R_{ab\mid NO} be the expected abort-branch losses conditional on the decision.

The loss of a GO decision and the loss of a NO-GO decision are

L\left(GO\right)=\left(1-p\right)C_U+R_{ab\mid GO}

L\left(NO\text{-}GO\right)=p\,V_R+R_{ab\mid NO}

Capture is authorized when L(GO)<L(NO\text{-}GO), which yields

\boxed{\ GO\iff p>\frac{C_U+R_{ab\mid GO}-R_{ab\mid NO}}{C_U+V_R}\ }

The rule is correct in both limits. If the abort branch is identical in both states, R_{ab\mid GO}=R_{ab\mid NO}=R_{ab}, the threshold reduces to p>C_U/(C_U+V_R). If abort is available only after NO-GO, R_{ab\mid GO}=0 and R_{ab\mid NO}=R_{ab}, the threshold reduces to p>(C_U-R_{ab})/(C_U+V_R), and a risky abort lowers the bar for GO. If abort is dangerous after NO-GO but not after GO, the sign of the correction reverses. The framework requires both conditional costs to be estimated, not assumed.

A hard constraint remains enforced by the monitor:

P\left(\text{unsafe}\mid GO\right)\le\epsilon_U

so that the expected-loss rule cannot authorize a capture whose worst-case risk exceeds the cap regardless of expected value.

5.4 Abort admissibility is computed, not assumed

An offshore divert is not automatically safe. It requires its own state estimate, its own envelope, and its own probability of admissible outcome. The quantities R_{ab\mid GO} and R_{ab\mid NO} are computed from the abort model of the relevant vehicle class and are not set to zero by convenience.

