6. Learning-Enabled Margin Estimation under Runtime Assurance

6.1 Scope

The module estimates a recovery margin and its uncertainty and supplies that estimate to the decision logic of §5. It is advisory. It does not control the vehicle, does not replace guidance, and does not hold flight-termination authority.

6.2 Architectural stratification

The architecture is stratified into four operational roles.

Role Function Authority
L1 Estimation Learned margin and dispersion prediction with uncertainty Advisory only
L2 Monitor Verified constraints, input validation, switching Authorizes or rejects
L3 Control Deterministic guidance, control, and contact loop Executes within limits
L4 Protection Independent interlocks and flight termination Outside L1 and L2

This four-role stratification is an operational convention of this framework. The underlying monitor-switch-backup logic is the Simplex pattern. The stratification is not presented as a standard taxonomy.

6.3 Monitor specification

The monitor is deterministic. It is a reachability or interval checker over a safe set

\mathcal{S}=\left\{x:h(x)\ge 0\right\}

with dynamics \dot x=f(x)+g(x)u+w and disturbance bound w\in\mathcal{W}. A candidate robust barrier condition is

\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\ \inf_{w\in\mathcal{W}}\left[\nabla h^{\mathsf T}\left(f+gu+w\right)+\alpha\left(h\right)\right]\ge 0

with applicability established for the actual dynamics, sampling period, and estimation error; where it is not established, reachability or viability kernels are used instead.

The monitor is required to satisfy a timing condition:

T_{\text{monitor}}+T_{\text{detect}}+T_{\text{backup}}\le T_{\text{violation}}

where T_{\text{violation}} is the time-to-violation margin from the current state. The condition is evaluated against the worst-case admissible value or against a stated probabilistic bound whose justification is given explicitly. It is not evaluated against a mean. Latency distribution, false-positive rate, and false-negative rate are deliverables of the monitor's characterization, not assumptions.

6.4 H8a: learned margin prediction

Target. The target quantity is P_{\text{reach}}, not P_{\text{rec}}. The learned predictor is tested on whether it helps the vehicle reach a recoverable envelope, because that is the decision the upstream chain actually makes.

Hypothesis. A learned margin predictor under an independent monitor raises P_{\text{reach}} relative to a fixed-threshold baseline at matched false-authorization rate and at a false-refusal rate no worse than a pre-registered bound.

Procedure, in order. (i) Calibrate both predictors to a matched false-authorization rate. (ii) Freeze the operating point. (iii) Test

\Delta P_{\text{reach}}=P_{\text{reach}}^{\text{ML+RTA}}-P_{\text{reach}}^{\text{base}}>0

subject to

\mathrm{FNG}^{\text{ML+RTA}}\le \mathrm{FNG}^{\text{base}}+\delta_N

with the full receiver-operating-characteristic curve reported. Once calibration is exact, the false-authorization constraint is redundant as an independent test and is reported as a check; the binding constraint is the false-refusal bound, which prevents the predictor from passing by learning to say no.

False-authorization and false-refusal definitions. Let \mathcal{D} be the set of decision instances, each with a ground-truth label indicating whether a safe capture was in fact available. The false-authorization rate is the proportion of unsafe-to-capture instances on which capture was authorized; the false-refusal rate is the proportion of feasible instances on which capture was refused. Both are defined on the same reference population, with the same denominator, and the denominators are reported.

Input ablation. Two input sets are compared. The kinematics-only set contains vehicle state estimates and their derivatives. The augmented set adds propulsion-side observables: chamber-pressure residuals, mixture-ratio proxies, and integrated mass-flow estimates. Because engine telemetry is not public, the propulsion observables are synthetic and carry Tier D; the H8a result is therefore conditional on the assumed dispersion model, and that conditionality is part of the hypothesis, not a footnote.

The ablation tests whether propulsion observables add lead time, not whether they would have prevented any historical anomaly. The historical attribution of the Booster anomaly to propellant mass is recorded, and the question of whether guidance "did not know" is not treated as established.

Lead time and the actionable window. Lead time has operational value only if an action exists when the signal arrives. Define the actionable set \mathcal{T}_{\text{act}} as the set of instants at which a discrete action is available: boostback-duration adjustment, landing-burn throttle authority, divert commit, or capture-arm repositioning. The lead time is

\boxed{\ \tau_{\text{lead}}=t_{\text{deadline}}-t_{\text{flag}}\ }

where t_{\text{deadline}} is the latest instant at which the relevant action can still be taken. Positive \tau_{\text{lead}} means an action remains available; zero means the signal arrives at the limit; negative means the signal arrives too late. When several actions are admissible, the framework distinguishes the deadline of the action actually selected from the latest deadline over all admissible actions, and reports both, because they answer different operational questions. A predictor that flags the margin after the last actionable deadline has zero operational value regardless of its accuracy.

Rejection conditions. The hypothesis is rejected if \Delta P_{\text{reach}} is not meaningful; if the false-refusal rate exceeds \delta_N; if uncertainty intervals are miscalibrated; if performance degrades under out-of-distribution cases; if the gain vanishes when compute and information inputs are equalized; or if the propulsion-observable ablation adds no lead time inside \mathcal{T}_{\text{act}}.

6.5 H8b: graceful degradation

Decision performance must degrade within a pre-registered bound under link latency, packet loss, stale data, single-estimator failure, and contradictory predictions. Link loss must not by itself authorize capture or trigger termination; it triggers a local safety assessment followed by a verified fallback chosen from the last validated state, the uncertainty growth since that state, and the available contingency trajectories. The hypothesis is rejected if a single estimator fault produces an unsafe authorization, if stale data go undetected, if the fallback leaves the verified envelope, or if degradation exceeds its bound.

6.6 Common-mode analysis

Separate processes are not independent evidence. Shared sensors, shared training data, shared architecture, shared preprocessing, shared communication links, and shared dependencies are analysed explicitly. The monitor is not permitted to rely exclusively on estimates that the learned function also uses. Any independence claim is supported by a common-cause analysis rather than by process separation alone.

6.7 Timing and operational phases

Learned estimation operates in the coast and decision phases. The final approach and contact phases run on validated deterministic loops. Three configurations are compared: a fixed-threshold deterministic baseline; a single learned estimator under monitoring; and a specialized estimator ensemble under monitoring. Controls include information, compute, latency, model complexity, training data, scenario distribution, and monitor implementation. Numeric ensembles require their own calibration and conformal evidence; evidence from language-model agent benchmarks is not transferable and is not used as support.

6.8 Metrics

The module reports P_{\text{reach}}, P_{\text{rec}}, P_{\text{safe}}, the two abort-branch probabilities P_2 and P_3, false-authorization, false-refusal, calibration, latency, \tau_{\text{lead}}, robustness under fault and link loss, and compute cost.

