7. Comparative Optimization

7.1 Internal optimization before comparison

Each architecture is optimized internally over its control and actuation trajectory u before any cross-architecture comparison is made. Comparing an optimized architecture to an unoptimized one is not a comparison of architectures.

7.2 Four-objective Pareto front

The front is

J\left(\mathcal{A}\right)=\min_{u}\left[-P_{\text{rec}},\ \mathrm{CVaR}_{\beta}\left(L_{\text{peak}}\right),\ m_{\text{interface}},\ C_{\text{ground}}\right]

The remaining quantities of interest—expected fatigue damage, expected turnaround time, the abort-branch probabilities—are handled as \epsilon-constraints:

\mathbb{E}\left[D_{\text{fat}}\right]\le \epsilon_D,\qquad \mathbb{E}\left[T_{\text{turnaround}}\right]\le \epsilon_T,\qquad P_2\le \epsilon_2

A front of seven objectives is not interpretable by a decision-maker; four is at the limit of what can be read. P_{\text{reach}} is reported alongside the front but is not a Pareto axis, because it is an upstream property of the chain rather than a downstream property of the capture architecture.

7.3 Hard chance constraints

The following constraints are enforced as hard feasibility conditions, not traded on the front:

P\left(L_{\text{peak}}\le L_{\text{allow}}\right)\ge 1-\epsilon_L

P\left(\text{stable support}\right)\ge 1-\epsilon_S

P\left(\text{unsafe capture}\right)\le \epsilon_U

Risk tolerances \epsilon_L,\epsilon_S,\epsilon_U come from requirements, not from values that flatter a design.

7.4 Matched actuation envelopes

Capture-surface actuation bandwidth and stroke are parameters for every family, not a binary property that distinguishes "fixed" from "moving" architectures. The comparison is run both at each family's native actuation envelope and at a matched envelope, so that an architecture cannot appear superior merely because it was allowed more actuation authority.

