H8b Degradation remains within bound under fault and link loss Any rejection condition of §6.5

Pre-registration is a property of the run, not of the document: \delta_1, \delta_N, the minimum H8a gain, and the H8b degradation bound are fixed and timestamped before any comparison run, and they are expressed against the parameter box or its conditional value-at-risk so that the required effect must hold across the interval rather than at a nominal point.

---

9. Data Provenance and Claim Ledger

9.1 Parameter intervals

Every parameter is carried as an interval \left[p^{-},p^{+}\right] with source, retrieval date, method, vehicle configuration, and a measured-or-inferred flag. A point value without a provenance record is not used.

9.2 The claim ledger

The ledger is append-only and versioned. Its fields are: identifier, value or claim, tier, source, retrieval date, valid-from date, superseded-by, and reason. It covers causal claims as well as parameters.

The ledger is not a record of what the authors currently believe. It is a record of what was asserted, when, on what evidence, and what replaced it. A causal attribution that was revised remains in the ledger with a supersession link rather than being deleted. A load figure with no attributable source is removed as a design input, and the removal is recorded. A geometric dimension sourced only to secondary reconstruction is carried at Tier D with its retrieval date. A tolerance figure whose public sources conflict is carried as an interval with the conflict noted, and is not resolved by selecting the figure that is most convenient for the comparison.

---

10. Methodology and Sensitivity-First Gating

The work proceeds in seven stages: a sourced fact sheet and critical assessment of the reference architecture; the outcome-tree model of §5; a planar three-degree-of-freedom model with slosh, analytic limit tests, and convergence studies; architecture comparison under common scenarios and matched actuation envelopes; separate Ship and Booster models; spatial, flexible, multi-reservoir, nonlinear-contact, actuator, and tower models; robustness and validation. The learning module runs on the outputs of the second stage and is independent of the mechanical conclusions.

Gating. A reduced skeleton with interval parameters is run before the fact sheet is targeted at specific parameters. The skeleton sensitivity is run across at least two model forms—for example, rigid contact and compliant contact. A parameter ranked negligible must be negligible in both forms; otherwise the ranking certifies the skeleton's own omission. Free-play contact is a candidate mode in which a currently negligible parameter becomes dominant, and it is checked explicitly. This ordering matters because the fact sheet is expensive to assemble, and targeting it at the wrong parameters wastes that effort while producing a document that looks precise.

The rule that governs the whole programme is: no simulation may convert a poorly defined hypothesis into an apparently precise result.

---

11. Verification and Reproducibility

Four verification levels are distinguished: analytical, numerical, model validation, and engineering applicability. A result that is numerically converged but unvalidated is reported as such.

Monte Carlo estimation uses quasi-Monte Carlo sampling (Sobol sequences) with importance sampling or subset simulation for rare events. To bound a false-authorization rate at \epsilon with 95% confidence from zero observed events, the sample size is approximately

N\approx \frac{3}{\epsilon}

This is the three-over-n approximation to the one-sided binomial upper confidence bound under independent and identically distributed trials. It is a zero-event approximation; when events are permitted or a two-sided interval is required, the exact Clopper–Pearson interval or a bootstrap procedure is used instead. Bootstrap intervals are reported for all estimated rates.

Model versions, code versions, provenance records, random seeds, numerical tolerances, and outputs are stored. The implementation stack is open-source: Python, NumPy, SciPy, SymPy, CasADi, Project Chrono, CalculiX, and Git. Hobby-grade simulations are used only as comparison points and never as validated models.

---

12. Limitations

Public data are absent for mass histories, centre-of-mass evolution, tank geometry, slosh damping, fitting stiffness and clearance, allowable loads, actuator limits, guidance and abort thresholds, and contact telemetry. Propulsion-side observables used in the ablation are synthetic. Slosh evidence is sub-scale, and the extrapolation to full-scale vehicle parameters is a stated limitation. The runtime-assurance precedent cited is shadow-mode and small-satellite and provides no transferable quantitative evidence for a terminal contact decision. Tolerance figures for the net-capture benchmark conflict, and their comparison with rigid-capture precision is a category error that the framework avoids by using the admissible arrival-state set. The fitting load capacity is not publicly attributed and is not used as a design input. The results the framework can produce are comparative theoretical evidence under stated intervals; they are not certification evidence for any vehicle or ground system.

---

13. Expected Contributions

The framework is intended to produce, as objectives rather than as results: an integrated probabilistic outcome-tree model with separate reach, recovery, and safe-outcome probabilities and with abort-branch utilities distinguished by economic consequence; a progressive capture-dynamics model with contact-patch rocking and a model-form comparison for slosh against sub-scale data; a fair comparison of six capture families on a common admissible arrival-state basis and matched actuation envelopes; an explicit treatment of abort as a formal safety and economic decision entering both branches of the go/no-go rule; a chance-constrained, four-objective architecture selection with the remaining quantities as \epsilon-constraints; a testable learned-margin module under monitoring with input ablation, an action-window lead-time metric, and a correctly ordered two-sided comparison; and a reproducible open framework with an append-only ledger that records conflicting sources rather than resolving them by assertion.

Novelty is not claimed before a systematic literature and patent review.

---

14. Conclusion

Recovery is a coupled problem of dynamics, control, structures, and safety. The capture mechanism is one link. Whether a recovery system is better than an alternative is not determined by the precision of its nominal interface, nor by the elegance of its contact model, nor by the accuracy of its margin predictor in isolation. It is determined by whether the chain, taken as a whole and evaluated under stated uncertainty, terminates more often in acceptable leaf states—and by whether the economic cost of the leaf states it produces is acknowledged rather than hidden.

The framework developed here separates what can be predicted from what may be authorized and from what is executed. It treats the abort branch as a first-class outcome with a cost in both branches of the decision, defines the admissible arrival-state set as the common basis of architectural comparison, replaces a scalar slosh excitation with a vector field in the tank frame, replaces a fixed hinge axis with contact-patch rocking governed by the full rigid-body equations, and holds learned estimation under a deterministic monitor whose timing condition is stated against the worst admissible case rather than the mean. It carries its own evidence tiers and its own revision history, and it declines to invent the parameters that public evidence does not supply.

A theoretical framework of this kind does not prove that any particular architecture is superior. It specifies what would have to be true for such a claim to be supported, what would falsify it, and what remains unknown until better data exist. That is the contribution it can honestly claim.

