Appendix B — Variable-Mass Dynamics: Derivation and Consistency Check

B.1 Translational equation

At time t, the system consists of the vehicle and the propellant instantaneously within it, with total mass m(t) and centre-of-mass velocity \mathbf v(t). Over dt, the vehicle mass changes by dm<0, its velocity by d\mathbf v, and the expelled propellant of mass -dm leaves at velocity \mathbf v+\mathbf v_e in the inertial frame, where \mathbf v_e is the exhaust velocity relative to the vehicle.

Conservation of momentum in the absence of external forces gives

(m+dm)(\mathbf v+d\mathbf v)+(-dm)(\mathbf v+\mathbf v_e)=m\mathbf v

which, to first order, is m\,d\mathbf v-dm\,\mathbf v_e=\mathbf 0. With external force \mathbf F_{\text{ext}} and pressure thrust (p_e-p_a)A_e\mathbf n_e,

m\dot{\mathbf v}=\mathbf T+\mathbf F_{\text{ext}},\qquad
\mathbf T=-\dot m\,\mathbf v_e+(p_e-p_a)A_e\mathbf n_e.

The term -\dot m\,\mathbf v_e is the momentum flux; the pressure term is the nozzle pressure contribution. No other term carries the propellant momentum, so no double counting is possible provided the propellant momentum flux is not also included in \mathbf F_{\text{ext}}.

B.2 Rotational equation and the jet-damping correction

The angular momentum of the vehicle plus the propellant instantaneously within it is \mathbf H=\mathbf I(t)\boldsymbol\omega. Its rate of change is

\dot{\mathbf H}=\mathbf I\dot{\boldsymbol\omega}+\dot{\mathbf I}\boldsymbol\omega+\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf I\boldsymbol\omega,

where the last term is the gyroscopic contribution from the body-frame representation. The angular momentum balance on this control volume is

\dot{\mathbf H}=\mathbf M_{\text{ext}}+\mathbf M_{\text{thrust}},

with \mathbf M_{\text{ext}} the external moment (aero, gravity gradient, contact, slosh reaction) and \mathbf M_{\text{thrust}} the thrust moment about the centre of mass.

The term \dot{\mathbf I}\boldsymbol\omega is the jet-damping moment. It is the rate at which the propellant that leaves the tank carries away the rotational angular momentum the propellant possessed at the instant of exit. Because \dot{\mathbf I}\prec 0 along the rotation, this term reduces the angular acceleration and therefore damps the rotation.

Correction to the main text. The formulation in the main text carries both \dot{\mathbf I}\boldsymbol\omega and a separate \mathbf M_{\text{jet}}. These are the same quantity: \mathbf M_{\text{jet}}=-\dot{\mathbf I}\boldsymbol\omega. Writing both double-counts the jet damping. The correct form is

\boxed{\ \mathbf I(t)\dot{\boldsymbol\omega}+\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf I(t)\boldsymbol\omega+\dot{\mathbf I}(t)\boldsymbol\omega=\mathbf M_T+\mathbf M_{\text{aero}}+\mathbf M_c+\mathbf M_{\text{slosh}}\ }

with \dot{\mathbf I}\boldsymbol\omega identified as the jet-damping term, and no separate \mathbf M_{\text{jet}}. Equivalently, one may move the term to the right as \mathbf M_{\text{jet}}=-\dot{\mathbf I}\boldsymbol\omega, but not both.

B.3 Zero-slosh consistency check

In the limit of no slosh (\mathbf M_{\text{slosh}}\to 0), the equations must reduce to a single rigid body with time-varying inertia. Substituting \mathbf M_{\text{slosh}}=0 in the corrected rotational equation and using \mathbf H=\mathbf I\boldsymbol\omega, the equation is exactly \dot{\mathbf H}=\mathbf M_{\text{ext}}+\mathbf M_T, which is the rigid-body balance. The translational equation reduces to the rocket equation. The consistency check is analytical and is completed before any coupled simulation is run. If a numerical implementation does not reproduce this limit to machine precision, the implementation is rejected regardless of how plausible its coupled results appear.

B.4 Slosh reaction moment

The slosh reaction moment \mathbf M_{\text{slosh}} is the moment exerted by the sloshing fluid on the tank walls. It is not the moment of the fluid about the vehicle centre of mass, which is already part of \mathbf H through the fluid's contribution to \mathbf I(t). Including the fluid's orbital moment twice would double-count. The reaction moment is computed from the slosh model's generalized forces and is added once to the rotational equation.

