Appendix C — Slosh Excitation in the Tank Frame

C.1 Inertial acceleration of a tank-fixed point

A point at position \mathbf r in the tank frame, which rotates with the vehicle at angular velocity \boldsymbol\omega, has inertial acceleration

\mathbf a_{\text{inertial}}=\mathbf a_{\text{cm}}+\dot{\boldsymbol\omega}\times\mathbf r+\boldsymbol\omega\times(\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf r)+2\boldsymbol\omega\times\dot{\mathbf r}+\ddot{\mathbf r},

where \mathbf a_{\text{cm}} is the vehicle centre-of-mass acceleration and \dot{\mathbf r},\ddot{\mathbf r} are the velocity and acceleration relative to the tank frame. This is the standard rigid-body composition of accelerations.

C.2 Apparent acceleration felt by the fluid

The fluid equation of motion in the tank frame is

\rho\left(\frac{\partial\dot{\mathbf r}}{\partial t}+\dot{\mathbf r}\cdot\nabla\dot{\mathbf r}\right)=-\nabla p+\rho\mathbf g_{\text{tank}}+\rho\mathbf a_{\text{app}},

where \mathbf g_{\text{tank}}=\mathbf R^{\mathsf T}\mathbf g is gravity in the tank frame and \mathbf a_{\text{app}} collects the non-inertial contributions. From the translational equation m\mathbf a_{\text{cm}}=\mathbf T+\mathbf F_{\text{aero}}+\mathbf F_c+m\mathbf g, the non-inertial term is

\mathbf a_{\text{app}}=\mathbf R^{\mathsf T}\!\left(\frac{\mathbf T+\mathbf F_{\text{aero}}+\mathbf F_c}{m}+\dot{\boldsymbol\omega}\times\mathbf r+\boldsymbol\omega\times(\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf r)+2\boldsymbol\omega\times\dot{\mathbf r}\right).

The effective gravity is \mathbf g_{\text{eff}}=\mathbf g_{\text{tank}}-\mathbf a_{\text{app}}, and it is \mathbf g_{\text{eff}} that appears in the fluid momentum balance.

C.3 Why the scalar magnitude is insufficient

The main text's earlier scalar a_{\text{eff}}(t)=\|\mathbf T+\mathbf F_{\text{aero}}+\mathbf F_c\|/m captures only the first term inside the parenthesis of §C.2. It omits three contributions that matter for a directionally sensitive slosh model:

1. Direction. The slosh response depends on the component of \mathbf a_{\text{app}} perpendicular to the nominal free surface, not on its magnitude. A purely axial acceleration excites a different mode than an equal-magnitude lateral acceleration.
2. Euler term. \dot{\boldsymbol\omega}\times\mathbf r is the tangential acceleration from angular acceleration; it couples pitch or yaw acceleration to slosh.
3. Centripetal and Coriolis terms. \boldsymbol\omega\times(\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf r) and 2\boldsymbol\omega\times\dot{\mathbf r} couple roll rate and relative fluid motion to the apparent acceleration; they are non-negligible in a spinning vehicle or during a fast slew.

The scalar magnitude may be retained only as a conservative amplitude envelope for bounding, and must be labelled as such. It is not an adequate excitation for a directionally sensitive model.

C.4 Slosh generalized force

For the pendulum model with generalized coordinate \alpha and sensitive axis \mathbf e_\perp perpendicular to the nominal free surface,

Q_\alpha=m_s l_s\,\mathbf e_\perp^{\mathsf T}\mathbf a_{\text{app}}\cos\alpha,

and the equation of motion follows from the Lagrange derivation in §4.4 of the main text with a_{\text{app},\perp}=\mathbf e_\perp^{\mathsf T}\mathbf a_{\text{app}}. The scalar form is recovered only if \mathbf a_{\text{app}} is entirely along \mathbf e_\perp, which is not the general case.

