Appendix D — Contact Law: Properties, Energy Dissipation, and Restitution

D.1 The Hunt–Crossley law

The normal force at contact i is

F_{n,i}=K_i\,\delta_i^{\,n}\left[1+\frac{3(1-c_{r,i})}{2}\frac{\dot\delta_i}{\dot\delta_i^{(-)}}\right],\qquad F_{n,i}\leftarrow\max(0,F_{n,i}),

with \delta_i\ge 0, c_{r,i}\in[0,1], and \dot\delta_i^{(-)}>0 the approach velocity at contact initiation. The damping parameter is

\lambda_i=\frac{3(1-c_{r,i})}{2}\frac{K_i}{\dot\delta_i^{(-)}}.

D.2 Three properties that are provable

P1 — Vanishing damping at contact initiation. At \delta_i=0, \delta_i^{\,n}=0 for n>0, so F_{n,i}=0 regardless of the bracket. No non-physical tensile force is generated at the instant of contact.

P2 — No adhesion during unloading. The \max(0,\cdot) clamp ensures F_{n,i}\ge 0 at all times. During unloading, \dot\delta_i<0 makes the bracket smaller but never negative for c_{r,i}\le 1; the clamp then removes any residual negative force. The combined property is that the law cannot pull the bodies together.

P3 — Energy dissipation is non-negative for c_{r,i}<1. The damping force F_{d,i}=K_i\delta_i^{\,n}\lambda_i\dot\delta_i is proportional to \dot\delta_i. The work done by the damping force over a contact episode is

W_{d}=\int F_{d,i}\,\dot\delta_i\,dt=\int K_i\lambda_i\,\delta_i^{\,n}\,\dot\delta_i^{2}\,dt\ge 0,

with equality only if \dot\delta_i\equiv 0. For c_{r,i}<1, the coefficient is strictly positive, so energy is dissipated. The maximum dissipation is at c_{r,i}=0 (fully plastic), and the minimum is at c_{r,i}=1 (elastic limit, zero dissipation).

D.3 The restitution coefficient is a derived quantity

The Hunt–Crossley law is constructed so that c_{r,i} is the limiting coefficient of restitution for small damping. The exact restitution coefficient e_i=\dot\delta_i^{(+)}/\dot\delta_i^{(-)} for general damping is obtained by integrating the equation of motion

m\ddot\delta_i+F_{n,i}(\delta_i,\dot\delta_i)=0

over the compression and restitution phases, and it depends on n, K_i, m, and \dot\delta_i^{(-)} in addition to c_{r,i}. For n=1 and small damping, e_i\approx c_{r,i}; for n\neq 1 or large damping, the discrepancy is not negligible and is carried as a model-form uncertainty in the sensitivity analysis. This is an honest limitation of the law, not a defect specific to this framework.

D.4 Unit and sign check

The units of K_i are \mathrm{N\,m^{-n}}. The units of \dot\delta_i^{(-)} are \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}. The units of \lambda_i are therefore \mathrm{N\,m^{-n}\,s\,m^{-1}}=\mathrm{N\,s\,m^{-(n+1)}}, and the product \lambda_i\dot\delta_i is dimensionless in the bracket, as required. The sign of \lambda_i is fixed by c_{r,i}\le 1: for c_{r,i}\in[0,1], the coefficient (3/2)(1-c_{r,i})\ge 0, and with K_i>0 and \dot\delta_i^{(-)}>0, \lambda_i\ge 0. The sign convention for \dot\delta_i then determines whether the damping term adds to or subtracts from the elastic term; in the convention of §D.1, \dot\delta_i>0 during compression adds to the force, and \dot\delta_i<0 during restitution subtracts from it. This is the physically correct direction for a dissipative contact.

