Appendix G — Lead-Time Metric and Actionable Window

G.1 Actionable set

Let \mathcal T_{\text{act}}\subset\mathbb R be the set of instants at which at least one discrete action is available. Actions in scope: boostback-duration adjustment, landing-burn throttle authority, divert commit, and capture-arm repositioning. The set is determined by the vehicle's guidance authority and the ground segment's committed resources, and it is not assumed to be the entire flight.

G.2 Lead time

For a predictor that flags at t_{\text{flag}} and an action with latest usable instant t_{\text{deadline}},

\boxed{\ \tau_{\text{lead}}=t_{\text{deadline}}-t_{\text{flag}}\ }.

Sign convention: \tau_{\text{lead}}>0 means an action remains available; \tau_{\text{lead}}=0 means the signal arrives at the limit; \tau_{\text{lead}}<0 means the signal arrives too late. The earlier sign convention \tau_{\text{lead}}=t_{\text{flag}}-t_{\text{deadline}} made the opposite assignment and is superseded by this appendix.

G.3 Two deadlines, two questions

When several actions are admissible, two quantities answer different questions:

· t_{\text{deadline}}^{\text{selected}}: the latest usable instant of the action actually selected by the decision logic. This answers: does the predictor's signal arrive in time for the chosen action?
· t_{\text{deadline}}^{\max}: the latest usable instant among all admissible actions. This answers: is the signal arriving at any useful time regardless of which action is selected?

Both are reported. A predictor that flags after t_{\text{deadline}}^{\max} has zero operational value. A predictor that flags between t_{\text{deadline}}^{\text{selected}} and t_{\text{deadline}}^{\max} has value only if the decision logic is willing to switch actions. The framework does not conflate the two.

G.4 Operational interpretation

The lead-time distribution is reported separately for the kinematics-only and augmented input sets of §6.4. The H8a rejection condition "adds no lead time inside \mathcal T_{\text{act}}" is evaluated on the selected-action deadline, because that is the deadline the operational decision must meet.

