Appendix H — Statistical Confidence Bounds

H.1 Clopper–Pearson exact upper bound for zero events

Let X\sim\mathrm{Binomial}(n,p). The exact 100(1-\alpha)\% one-sided upper confidence bound for p given X=0 is

p_{\text{upper}}=1-\alpha^{1/n}.

This is a derived result: the coverage probability P_p(X=0\mid p\le p_{\text{upper}})=(1-p_{\text{upper}})^n=\alpha at the boundary, so the bound is exact for the binomial model under independent and identically distributed trials.

H.2 The three-over-n approximation

For large n and small \alpha^{1/n},

-\ln\alpha^{1/n}=-\frac{\ln\alpha}{n}\approx p_{\text{upper}},\qquad
-\ln\alpha=\begin{cases}2.996\approx 3,&\alpha=0.05,\\4.605\approx 4.6,&\alpha=0.01,\end{cases}

so p_{\text{upper}}\approx 3/n for 95% confidence and \approx4.6/n for 99% confidence. The "rule of three" is therefore the 95% zero-event approximation, not a universal constant. It is valid under (i) zero observed events, (ii) independent identically distributed trials, (iii) the binomial model. When events are permitted or a two-sided interval is required, the exact Clopper–Pearson bound (for general X) or a bootstrap procedure is used instead.

H.3 Sample size for the H8a false-refusal bound

The false-refusal bound \delta_N is tested by the same machinery. To assert that \mathrm{FNG}\le\delta_N with 95% confidence under zero observed false refusals, n_{\text{FNG}}\approx3/\delta_N independent reference instances are required. The reference population and denominator are stated in §6.4 of the main text; the sample size is derived from the claimed bound, not the other way round.

H.4 Bootstrap intervals

For estimated rates that are not at the zero-event boundary, percentile bootstrap intervals with B\ge10^4 resamples are reported. The bootstrap resampling unit is the trajectory, not the time step, to preserve within-trajectory correlation.

