Appendix I — Monitor Timing and the Robust Barrier Condition

I.1 Timing condition

The monitor satisfies

T_{\text{monitor}}+T_{\text{detect}}+T_{\text{backup}}\le T_{\text{violation}}

where T_{\text{monitor}} is the sampling and computation latency, T_{\text{detect}} is the detection delay from a violation onset to the monitor's output, and T_{\text{backup}} is the time for the verified backup to take authority. T_{\text{violation}} is the time-to-violation margin from the current state. The condition is evaluated at the worst-case admissible value of each term, not at the mean. Where a probabilistic bound is used instead, the bound is explicitly justified by a tail estimate and is reported alongside the distribution from which it was derived. A monitor that satisfies the condition only in expectation does not satisfy the condition.

I.2 Robust barrier condition

Let \mathcal S=\{x:h(x)\ge0\} with h:\mathcal X\to\mathbb R continuously differentiable. The robust barrier condition is

\sup_{u\in\mathcal U}\inf_{w\in\mathcal W}\left[\nabla h^{\mathsf T}\big(f(x)+g(x)u+w\big)+\alpha\big(h(x)\big)\right]\ge0\qquad\forall x\in\mathcal S,

with \alpha a class-\mathcal K function. The condition ensures that for every disturbance w\in\mathcal W there exists a control u such that \dot h\ge-\alpha(h) along the closed loop. By Nagumo's invariance theorem and the comparison lemma, h(0)\ge0\Rightarrow h(t)\ge0 for all t\ge0. The safe set is forward invariant under the selected control.

I.3 Applicability obligations

The barrier condition is a sufficient condition for invariance only if:

1. f,g are locally Lipschitz on \mathcal S;
2. the disturbance set \mathcal W is compact and the worst case is attained or approached within the sampling interval;
3. the estimate \hat x used by the monitor satisfies an error bound \|x-\hat x\|\le e_x whose effect is incorporated into a tightened safe set \mathcal S_{e_x}\subseteq\mathcal S;
4. the numerical implementation of the supremum and infimum is verified against analytical test cases.

Where (1)–(4) cannot be established, a reachability or viability kernel computation is used instead, and the monitor's guarantees are stated as conservative with respect to the underlying dynamics. The monitor is required to be simpler and better characterized than the estimator it supervises; an uncharacterized monitor does not satisfy this requirement regardless of its architecture.

---

Appendix J — Pareto Front and \epsilon-Constraint Equivalence

J.1 The multi-objective problem

The front is

\min_{u\in\mathcal U}\ \big(J_1(u),\dots,J_k(u)\big)

with J_1=-P_{\text{rec}}, J_2=\mathrm{CVaR}_\beta(L_{\text{peak}}), J_3=m_{\text{interface}}, J_4=C_{\text{ground}}, and the remaining quantities as \epsilon-constraints:

\mathbb E[D_{\text{fat}}]\le\epsilon_D,\quad \mathbb E[T_{\text{turnaround}}]\le\epsilon_T,\quad P_2\le\epsilon_2.

J.2 \epsilon-constraint equivalence

For a feasible set \mathcal U and objective vector J, a point u^\star is Pareto-optimal for the constrained problem if and only if it is optimal for the \epsilon-constraint problem

\min_{u\in\mathcal U}\ J_1(u)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad J_j(u)\le\epsilon_j,\ j=2,\dots,k,

for some \epsilon=(\epsilon_2,\dots,\epsilon_k) that is binding at u^\star. This is the standard \epsilon-constraint theorem. The equivalence holds for convex problems; for non-convex problems the \epsilon-constraint method generates a subset of the Pareto front, not necessarily all of it. This caveat is stated in the main text and applies here. The four-objective front is therefore a partial characterization of the Pareto set; it is the part that the \epsilon-constraint construction can reach under the problem's non-convexity. Additional Pareto points, if they exist, are not claimed.

J.3 Why four objectives

A front of seven objectives is not interpretable by a decision-maker. Four is at the limit of what can be read on a single chart with axis pairs, and it matches the number of quantities the decision-maker can weigh simultaneously without a formal preference model. The remaining quantities are constraints precisely because they are not tradeable against the four axes; they define the feasible region, not the objective. Merging them into the axes would invite trades that the requirements do not permit.

J.4 Hard chance constraints

The chance constraints of §7.3 are feasibility conditions, not objectives:

P(L_{\text{peak}}\le L_{\text{allow}})\ge1-\epsilon_L,\qquad
P(\text{stable support})\ge1-\epsilon_S,\qquad
P(\text{unsafe capture})\le\epsilon_U.

A candidate that violates any of these is infeasible regardless of its position on the four-objective front. The tolerances \epsilon_L,\epsilon_S,\epsilon_U come from the requirements, not from the objectives, and are pre-registered with the hypothesis thresholds of §8.

