Appendix K — Claim Ledger: Specification and Format

K.1 Purpose

The ledger records every parameter and every causal claim, with provenance, date, and supersession history. It is append-only: entries are never deleted, only superseded. A first-day attribution that is later revised remains in the ledger with a superseded_by link. This is what makes the record auditable when the public narrative changes within hours.

K.2 Fields

Field Content Example
ID Unique identifier FL15-CAUSE
value_or_claim The asserted value or causal statement "boostback cutoff caused by low fuel mass"
tier A/B/C/D per §3.1 C
source Citation with retrieval date "secondary reporting, 2026-11-14"
retrieval_date Date the source was accessed 2026-11-14
valid_from Date the claim was first asserted 2026-11-15
superseded_by ID of the superseding entry, or empty FL15-CAUSE-v2
reason Reason for revision, if any "initial GNC attribution retracted"

K.3 Coverage

The ledger covers parameters (numerical inputs) and causal claims (statements about what caused what). The Flight 15 cause revision is a causal claim and is recorded as such. The "50 tons per side" figure is a parameter and, when removed as a design input, is recorded as removed with the reason. A geometric dimension sourced only to secondary reconstruction is a parameter at Tier D. A tolerance figure whose public sources conflict is a parameter recorded as an interval with the conflict noted.

K.4 Not a belief record

The ledger records what was asserted and on what evidence, not what the authors currently believe. The current belief is a derived quantity: it is the set of entries with superseded_by empty. This separation is what makes the record usable when the belief changes.

---

Appendix L — Parameter Interval Schema

L.1 Fields per parameter

Each parameter p is carried as

p=\big[p^{-},p^{+}\big]

with the following metadata:

Field Content
p_low, p_high Interval endpoints
tier A/B/C/D
source Citation
retrieval_date Date accessed
method Measured, inferred, reconstructed, or secondary
vehicle_config Which vehicle or test article
notes Known conflicts, dependencies, or caveats

L.2 Tier-dependent use

A Tier A or B parameter may be used as a model input with its stated uncertainty. A Tier C parameter is used with sensitivity analysis and its effect on the conclusion is reported. A Tier D parameter is used only in exploratory runs and is never a design input. A parameter that is central to a conclusion but available only at Tier D renders the conclusion conditional, and this conditionality is part of the result.

L.3 No point estimates without intervals

A parameter without an interval is not used. Where the public evidence supplies a single number, the interval is the smallest interval consistent with the source's stated precision. Where the source is silent on precision, the interval is widened to reflect the unknown precision, and the widening is recorded.

L.4 Conflict retention

When two sources disagree, the interval spans both and the conflict is recorded in notes. The framework does not resolve the conflict by selecting the figure that is most convenient for a conclusion. The admissible arrival-state set of the net-capture benchmark is the paradigm case (§3.6 of the main text): the tolerance figures conflict, and the conflict is retained rather than resolved by choosing 10 m or ±50 m.

